''tute, toute''), prob. < MD. ''tuyten'', D. ''tuiten'', also ''toeten'' = MLG. ''tuten'', sound a horn, = OHG. ''diozan'', MHG. ''diezen'', make a loud noise, = Icel. ''thjōta'', whistle as the wind, sough, resound, = AS. ''theótan'', howl, make a noise, = Sw. ''tjuta'', howl, = Dan. ''tude'', howl, blow a horn; cf. D. ''toet-horen'', a bugle-horn, MHG. ''duz'', m., noise, Icel. ''thytr'', noise, whistling wind, Goth. ''thuthaurn'', horn, trumpet; perhaps orig. imitative, as the later forms are regarded.] I. ''intrans.'' 1. To blow a horn, a whistle, or other wind-instrument; especially, to produce harsh or discordant sounds with a horn, cornet, trumpet, whistle, or the like.

{{smaller block|To ''Tute'' in a horne, cornucinere.
{{right|''Levins'', Manip. Vocab. (E. E. T. S.), p. 196.}}}}

{{smaller block|That foule musicke which a horne maketh, being ''tooted'' in. ''Chaloner'', tr. of Moriæ Encomium, H b. (''Nares''.)}}

2. To give out sound, as a wind-instrument when blown: usually a word of disparagement.

{{smaller block|O lady, I heard a wee horn ''toot'',
And it blew wonder clear.
{{right|''Lord Barnaby'' (Child's Ballads, II. 309).}}}}

{{smaller block|You are welcome to my thoughts; and these are, to part with the little ''tooting'' instrument in your jacket to the first fool you meet with.
{{right|''J. F. Cooper'', Last of Mohicans, xii.}}}}

3. To make sounds like those of a horn or a steam-whistle; trumpet.

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|We made a very happy escape from the elephants. They soon got our scent, raised their trunks, ''tooted'' as no locomotive could ''toot'', their ears sticking out straight, and off they went through the trees and tall grass.
{{right|''The Century'', XXXIX. 613.}}}}

'''4.''' Specifically, to call: said of some grouse.

{{smaller block|The [pinnated] Grouse in the spring commences about April to ''toot'', and can be heard nearly a mile.
{{right|''Sportsman's Gazetteer'', p. 124.}}}}

'''5.''' To whine; cry. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To sound on a horn, trumpet, pipe, or the like.

{{smaller block|Jockie, say, What might he be<br>That sits on yonder hill,<br>And ''tooteth'' out his notes of glee?
{{right|''W. Browne'', Shepherd's Pipe, ii.}}}}

'''2.''' To blow, as an instrument of sound.

{{smaller block|The elephant . . . turned and went down the hill, . . . ''tooting'' his trumpet as though in great fright.
{{right|''The Century'', XXXIX. 613.}}}}

'''toot'''<sup>2</sup> (töt), ''n.'' [< ''toot''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' A sound made by blowing on a wind-instrument; a note as of a horn; a blast.

{{smaller block|But I hae nae broo' of charges, since that awfu' morning that a ''tout'' of a horn, at the Cross of Edinburgh, blew half the faithfu' ministers of Scotland out of their pulpits.
{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xxxix.}}}}

{{smaller block|Go to the farthest end of the room and blow the pipe in gentle ''toots''.
{{right|''Mayer'', Sound, p. 78.}}}}

'''2.''' A blow-out; a spree: as, to go on a ''toot''. [Slang, U. S.]

'''toot'''<sup>3</sup> (töt), ''n.'' [Origin uncertain; cf. ''tout''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''1.''' A lazy, worthless person. [Slang.]

{{smaller block|Marsh Yates, the "shif'less ''toot'','' and his beautiful, energetic wife.
{{right|''Harper's Mag.'', LXXVII. 801.}}}}

